   273	and d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ for some |δ| < m̄_k. Then
   274	
   275	  |F_k(q)| ≤ Σ_{v,ℓ,|δ|<m̄_k} (P·H/(2·2^kY·T) + 1)
   276	           ≤ 2|V||𝒫_k| m̄_k·(PH/(2^{k+1}YT) + 1).
   277	
   278	*Proof.*
   279	- Fix (v, ℓ, δ) and let g′ = gcd(A − B, d) ≤ H (A ≠ B, |A − B| < H).
   280	- The congruence (A−B)(p−1) ≡ ℓ²δ (mod d) is either unsolvable, or holds exactly on one
   281	  class of p mod d/g′.
   282	- ℓ ∤ d, because clean means ℓ ∤ q − 1. With p ≡ AqB^{−1} (mod ℓ), CRT gives one class mod
   283	  ℓd/g′ ≥ ℓd/H.
   284	- An interval of length P/2 contains ≤ P H/(2ℓd) + 1 such integers. ∎
   285	
   286	## 4. Proof of Theorem D′
   287	
   288	**(i) Correctness.** Only gcds with N in (1, N), or band primes dividing N, are output.
   289	
   290	**(ii) Cost (bit operations; M(b) = O(b log b) multiplication; per block, with T_k
   291	targets and m ≤ m̄_k).**
   292	
   293	| step | operations | bit cost |
   294	|---|---|---|
   295	| prime sieve to 16Y, V, Lo_v/Hi_v (isqrt of O(n)-bit integers) | O(Y log log Y) + O(H²) isqrt | Õ(Y + H²n) |
   296	| integer product tree of ℓ² (Σ bits = log₂L_k ≤ 3·2^kY) | balanced tree | O(M(Y)·log Y) |
   297	| exponent tree (per level Σ exponent bits ≤ log₂L_k + #nodes; ⌈log₂|𝒫_k|⌉ levels) + γ, γ^{−1} | O(Y log Y) mults mod N + 1 inversion | O(Y log Y·M(n)) |
   298	| roots (Euler test, powering mod ℓ), s, c, windows | O(\|V\|\|𝒫_k\| log Y) mults mod ℓ, ℓ² | Õ(H²Y) |
   299	| w_ℓ = u_ℓ^N; giants (exponents of O(n) bits) | O(n\|𝒫_k\| + nT_k + T′) mults mod N | O((n(T_k + Y) + T′)·M(n)) |
   300	| choice of m (endpoint-competitive grid: O(n) grid points × T_k targets); inversions B^{−1} mod ℓ and r^{−1} mod ℓ²; reductions N mod ℓ, ℓ² | O(nT_k) word/small-integer ops | Õ(T_k n^{O(1)}) |
   301	| babies γ^i | m mults | O(m M(n)) |
   302	| f by product tree; remainder tree at the T′ = Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m⌉ ≤ T_k + Σ_τ W_τ/m giants | O((M(m) + M(T′)) log(m+T′)) ring ops; Kronecker substitution makes a degree-D product O(M(D·(2n + log D))) bits | Õ((m + T′)n) |
   303	| gcds | T′ gcds of n-bit numbers | O(T′ M(n) log n) |
   304	
   305	With m = m̄_k (or any m that minimises the list count) the list is
   306	m + T′ ≤ m̄_k + T_k + 1 ≤ 2.5HP/Y² + 4 + 2H²·2^{k+1}Y. Over the four blocks:
   307	
   308	  total = O(n^{O(1)}·(HP/Y² + H²Y)) = O(n^{O(1)} H^{5/3} P^{1/3}),
   309	
   310	because Y ≍ P^{1/3}H^{−1/3}. With H = n·2^{εn/2} this is O(n^{O(1)}·2^{(1/6+5ε/6)n}).
   311	
   312	**Arithmetic model (referee dclt_1, item 4).**
   313	- The bound assumes quasi-linear integer multiplication M(b) = O(b log b) (Harvey–van der
   314	  Hoeven; Schönhage–Strassen gives an extra log log). This applies to the exponent product
   315	  tree, the Kronecker-substituted polynomial products and the gcds.
   316	- Kronecker substitution over composite N: coefficients lie in [0, N), and the slots are
   317	  wider than 2n + ⌈log₂(D+1)⌉.
   318	- Monic remainders: reversal plus Newton inversion of a power series with constant term 1.
   319	  No inverse of a nonunit is ever needed.
   320	- The experimental metric E (§8) is a *proxy*. It is not the proof of (ii). The Python
   321	  implementation uses GMP integer products (gmpy2) and flint polynomial arithmetic. Its
   322	  `powN` charges binary square-and-multiply counts.
   323	
   324	**(iii) Exceptional set.**
   325	- Let T = ⌊2^{(1/4−ε)n}⌋. For (p,q) ∈ S_n, let k(q) be the least block clean for q
   326	  (Lemma 6). If
   327	  - (a) d = ord_q(2) > T,
   328	  - (b) p ∉ U_{k(q)}(q), and
   329	  - (c) p ∉ F_{k(q)}(q),
   330	
   331	  then DCL-det succeeds.
   332	- Indeed by (b) some target τ of block k(q) covers p (Lemma 1, coverage criterion).
   333	- By (c) and Lemma 7, its true giant is not FULL: the exact δ-range lies inside |δ| < m̄_k.
   334	- By Lemma 5 that giant's gcd is p. The algorithm outputs a nontrivial divisor at block k(q)
   335	  at the latest. It may output q, or output earlier from another block; both are fine.
   336	- So the FAIL pairs lie in E_a ∪ E_b ∪ E_c, with E_a = {ord_q(2) ≤ T},
   337	  E_b = {d > T, p ∈ U_{k(q)}(q)} and E_c = {d > T, p ∈ F_{k(q)}(q)}.
   338	
   339	The counts sum over prime q ∈ (P/√2, √2P), of which there are ≤ 3P/n for n large:
   340	
   341	- **|E_a|.** By Lemma 8 with Q₀ = 2^{(n−1)/2}, each bad q has ≤ P/2 + 1 partners, so
   342	  |E_a| ≤ (T(T+1)/(n−1))·(P/2+1) ≤ 2^{(1−2ε)n}/n·(1 + o(1)). Then
   343	  |E_a|/|S_n| ≤ (n/c₀)·2^{−2εn}(1 + o(1)).
   344	  - *Correction (dclt_1):* T(T+1) replaces T². The estimate is unchanged.
   345	- **|E_b|.** By Lemma 9, Σ_q |U_{k(q)}(q)| ≤ (3P/n)·(P/2 + 1 + 256Y²)·n/(c₁H²) ≤ C P²/H².
   346	  So |E_b|/|S_n| ≤ C n²/H² ≤ C 2^{−εn}. This is the audit's repaired normalisation: one
   347	  fixed interval, integers counted, no short-interval prime counts.
   348	- **|E_c|.** By Lemma 10 with |V| ≤ H², |𝒫_k| ≤ 2^kY and m̄_k ≤ 3HP/(4^kY²):
   349	  |F_k(q)| ≤ 6H⁴P²/(Y²T) + 6H³P/Y. So
   350	
   351	    |E_c|/|S_n| ≤ (3P/n)(6H⁴P²/(Y²T) + 6H³P/Y)·n²/(c₀P²) ≤ C n (H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T + H^{10/3}/P^{1/3}),
   352	
   353	  using Y² ≥ P^{2/3}H^{−2/3}. Here H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T ≤ (2n)^{14/3}2^{(−1/12 + 7ε/3 + ε)n} and
   354	  H^{10/3}/P^{1/3} ≤ n^{4}2^{(5ε/3 − 1/6)n}.
   355	- **Conclusion.** For ε ≤ 1/60, −1/12 + 10ε/3 ≤ −1/36 < −ε − (small). So |E_c|/|S_n| =
   356	  O(2^{−εn}) for n ≥ n₀(ε), and the total is O(2^{−εn}) = O(N^{−ε}). ∎
   357	
   358	**Corollary D″.**
   359	- Take H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉ and T = ⌊2^{n/4} n^{−1−a}⌋.
   360	- (a) gives O(n^{−2a−1}) and (b) gives O(n^{−2a}).
   361	- (c) gives n^{O(1)}·2^{−n/12}·n^{1+a}·n^{O(1)} = o(n^{−2a}).
   362	- The cost is n^{O(1)}H^{5/3}2^{n/6}. ∎
   363	
   364	**Where the constants come from.**
   365	- ε ≤ 1/60 is used only in (c) (the requirement is −1/12 + 13ε/3 < 0, i.e. ε < 1/52) and in
   366	  the scale separations Y > H² (ε < 1/7) and Y⁴ > √2P (ε < 1/4).
   367	- The exceptional rate N^{−ε} comes from the large sieve (b), which is the binding term.
   368	- The Poisson heuristic predicts e^{−λ} with λ ≍ H²/n. That would give N^{−ε} already at
   369	  H ≍ n√ε, i.e. polylog cost. The large sieve is the lossy step.
   370	
