   376	| (2) deterministic roots | band primes ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4), root a^{(ℓ+1)/4}; sieve loses a constant (PNT mod 4) | proved |
   377	| (3) bit complexity: balanced integer product tree; uncharged Bluestein powers | balanced tree (GMP products) in `all_but_one`; full cost table §4(ii). The HH baseline's Bluestein chirp powers are now charged (`stage3.harvey_find_collisions`) | proved (table); implemented |
   378	| (4) **base order of γ = β^L** (the substantive gap; 1333 example) | **Closed without any order lemma for β^L.** No order is needed mod p (Lemma 3 is an identity). Mod q, the four disjoint bands guarantee a clean band (Lemma 6), so ord_q(γ) = ord_q(2) (Lemma 7). Two-sided true giants are counted directly (Lemma 10), and small ord_q(2) is rare by an elementary count (Lemma 8). Extra failures are O(2^{−εn}), not o(1). The 1333 example (L built from 3, 5, 7 \| p−1, q−1) cannot occur in a clean band, and an L-power image of size 2 mod **p** is harmless here. | proved (+ GPT-6.1-sol referee dclt_1: "closes the previous order/extraction gap") |
   379	| (5) quantifier mismatch | §1: D′ concerns the true factor counted over S_n. It does not give full-candidate coverage for almost every N | stated |
   380	| (6) prior art | §10 | McKee–Pinch §5 read (r13 scans): direct precursor (heuristic N^{1/4}); the combination is claimed only as "no prior formulation found by us"; Carella 1308.2891 claim noted with the r13 assessment |
   381	| (7) experiments used greedy Hmax=12, not Thm D's design | §8 runs the theorem's algorithm (4 bands, ℓ ≡ 3 mod 4, base 2, fixed V(H), n-only Y) with an n-only H-schedule (λ₀ ≈ 3, i.e. H ≍ √n). The bridge: D′'s algorithm is defined for any n-only (H, Y), and the schedule H ≍ √n lies *below* Corollary D″'s range (a > 0, H = n^{1+a}). There the proved bound n²/H² is vacuous, so the experiment tests the algorithm, the lemmas and the cost, not the proved rate. The heuristic rate e^{−λ} is what the data reflect. | disclosed |
   382	
   383	Work items not covered above:
   384	- **Small-factor removal (item 3).** Unnecessary. On S_n both factors exceed P/2 > 16Y ≥ every
   385	  band prime, and the target is only S_n. Off the population the algorithm may FAIL (allowed),
   386	  and band primes dividing N are output.
   387	- **One-sided collisions (item 2, last bullet).** These are Lemmas 4, 5, 7 and 10. The weight
   388	  (1,1) is excluded because it is always two-sided.
   389	- **Restricting to M ∤ p−1 (item 2).** Not needed. Cleanliness is needed only for q − 1, and is
   390	  handled by trying 4 bands rather than by an exceptional set. Order mod p is irrelevant.
   391	- **Harvey–Hittmeir 2026 / Oznovich–Volk (item 2, first bullet).** Not used. The audit notes they
   392	  produce an arbitrary large-order element, not one in the L-power image. The clean-band argument
   393	  makes the L-power harmless mod q and irrelevant mod p.
   394	
   395	## 6. Where the result stops (precise remaining gaps)
   396	
   397	1. **Worst case (every N in S_n) is NOT proved.** Three independent obstacles remain:
   398	   - (a) **coverage** of every p, which is multimod's DCH (conjectured; the large sieve gives
   399	     only "almost all");
   400	   - (b) **q with small ord_q(2)** (≤ N^{1/4−ε}). The proof simply discards them (E_a). A
   401	     worst-case version would need a base with large order mod the specific prime q *on a
   402	     clean band*. Harvey–Hittmeir 2026 gives a large-order element mod N, but large order
   403	     mod N does not give large order mod q;
   404	   - (c) pairs where **every** covering true giant is FULL (E_c). These are rare on average,
   405	     but not excluded for a fixed N.
   406	2. **The exceptional rate is limited by the large sieve.** The proved fraction is n²/H², so
   407	   N^{−ε} costs H = N^{ε/2}, i.e. N^{5ε/6} in the cost. The Poisson heuristic, which the
   408	   multimod and §8 data support, predicts e^{−λ} with λ ≍ H²/n. That would give N^{−ε} at
   409	   polylog cost, but it is **conjectured**.
   410	3. **Ratios q/p > 2** are not covered (multimod §3.9).
   411	4. **Finite sizes.** At 40–80 bits HH is 2.3–4.9× cheaper in E (§8). The measured slope is
   412	   smaller (0.203 vs 0.226), and an extrapolated crossover lies near 130 bits. That is **not a
   413	   claim**: the slope difference partly reflects the H-schedule.
   414	5. **Below 1/6.** multimod Thm A (enlarged sumset model) is unchanged. Nothing here goes
   415	   below 1/6.
