   242	## 3. Consequences for Theorem D′ — proved
   243	
   244	**Theorem 2 (coverage exceptional set).**
   245	- Setting: the D′ design with any H satisfying 2 ≤ H ≤ N^{1/15}/C₀; n ≥ n₀; Y = ⌈2^{n/6}H^{−1/3}⌉;
   246	  four blocks 𝒫_k ⊂ (2^kY, 2^{k+1}Y] of primes ≡ 3 (mod 4).
   247	- E_b is the set of pairs of S_n uncovered by their clean block k(q).
   248	- Then
   249	
   250	  |E_b|/|S_n| ≤ C·n²·(λ₀^{−2} + H^{−6}) + C·n⁶H^{1/3}N^{−1/6} ≤ C′·n⁴/H⁴ + C′·n⁶H^{1/3}N^{−1/6}.
   251	
   252	*Proof.*
   253	- E_b ⊂ ∪_{k=0}^{3} {(p,q) ∈ S_n : W_k(p,q) = 0}.
   254	- Prime pairs of S_n lie in 𝔅 with X = ⌈√2·2^{n/2}⌉. They avoid ℒ (§1).
   255	- Apply Theorem 1 to each block, with Y_k = 2^kY ≥ Y.
   256	  - Hypotheses: Y ≥ 16H² ⟸ 2^{n/6} ≥ 16H^{7/3}, and HX < Y⁴ ⟸ H^{7/3} < 2^{n/6}/√2. Both
   257	    hold for H ≤ N^{1/15}/C₀.
   258	  - λ_k ≥ c₁H²/n by D′ Lemma 9. Take H ≥ C√n so that λ_k ≥ 1; otherwise the claim is trivial.
   259	- Use Corollary 1 at this scale:
   260	  - Y_k³ ≤ 512Y³ and Y³H ≤ 2P, so HY_k³/(|V|³X) = O(|V|^{−3}) = O(H^{−6});
   261	  - Y_k²/(Xs⁴) = O(n⁴P^{−1/3}H^{−2/3});
   262	  - 1/(Y_k s³) = O(n³P^{−1/3}H^{1/3});
   263	  - the remaining terms are smaller.
   264	- So #{uncovered in block k} ≤ X²[3λ_k^{−2} + λ_k^{−3} + O(H^{−6} + n⁴P^{−1/3}H^{1/3})].
   265	- Divide by |S_n| ≥ c₀P²/n². ∎
   266	
   267	**The explicit constants are poor.** `bound.scale_terms(n, H, k)` evaluates every
   268	normalised term at the D′ scale, and the error terms beat the main term only for large n.
   269	The dominant pieces are rows B₄ (shortest-vector sum) and F₄ (crude bound on 𝒟).
   270	
   271	| n | H | block | λ | 3/λ² + 1/λ³ | Σ errors (normalised) | D′ bound n²/H² (no constant) |
   272	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   273	| 96 | 32 | 0 | 21.7 | 6.5e−3 | 1.4e3 | 9.0 |
   274	| 200 | 2^{10} | 0 | 1.1e4 | 2.7e−8 | 3.2e−2 | 3.8e−2 |
   275	| 300 | 2^{15} | 0 | 7.3e6 | 5.7e−14 | 1.8e−6 | 8.4e−5 |
   276	| 400 | 2^{20} | 0 | 5.6e9 | 9.7e−20 | 1.3e−10 | 1.5e−7 |
   277	
   278	So Theorem 2 is an asymptotic improvement. At cryptographic-scale n the second term
   279	(n⁶H^{1/3}N^{−1/6}-type) dominates the proved bound. Even then the proved bound already beats
   280	D′'s n²/H² at n ≥ 300 in this table. Sharpening rows B and F, for example with smooth
   281	weights and an average over the shortest vectors, is routine but not done.
   282	
   283	**Proposition 4 (one-variable large sieve with a prime presieve; proved, standard).**
   284	|E_b|/|S_n| ≤ C/(1 + λ_min) = O(n/H²).
   285	
   286	*Proof.*
   287	- Fix a prime q and a block k. Let U(q) be the set of *prime* p ∈ (P/2, P] uncovered by block k.
   288	- Put Q = ⌊√P⌋ and Z = ⌊Q/2^{k+1}Y⌋. Then 1 ≤ Z < Y and ln Z ≥ (1/6 − o(1))·ln P.
   289	- U(q) avoids the |V| classes AqB^{−1} mod each ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k, and the class 0 mod each prime r ≤ Z,
   290	  since p > P/2 > Z.
   291	- The arithmetic large sieve (Montgomery 1968; Montgomery–Vaughan 1973) gives
   292	  |U(q)| ≤ (P/2 + 1 + Q²)/J.
   293	- In J, keep d = m and d = mℓ, with m ≤ Z squarefree and ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k.
   294	  - These d are ≤ Q and pairwise distinct, because the primes of m are < Y < ℓ.
   295	  - So J ≥ (Σ_{m≤Z} μ²(m)/φ(m))·(1 + Σ_ℓ |V|/(ℓ−|V|)) ≥ (1+λ_k)·ln Z.
   296	- Summing over the ≤ 3P/n primes q gives |E_b| ≤ C P²/(n²(1+λ)). ∎
   297	
   298	This is the r16 / sieve2d_1 refinement. I verified it line by line. It removes D′'s n-loss but
   299	not the power of λ.
   300	
   301	**Theorem 3 (cost/exception trade-off; proved).** Let ε′ ∈ (0, 1/80] and n ≥ n₀(ε′). The
   302	algorithm DCL-det_{n,ε′} of D′ §2, unchanged, has:
   303	- H = ⌈n·2^{ε′n/2}⌉;
   304	- cost N^{1/6+5ε′/6}(log N)^{O(1)};
   305	- at most C·2^{−2ε′n}·|S_n| FAIL pairs.
   306	
   307	Restated with ε = 2ε′: **N^{−ε} exceptions at cost N^{1/6+5ε/12}(log N)^{O(1)}, for
   308	ε ≤ 1/40.** Equivalently, cost N^{1/6+δ+o(1)} with an exceptional fraction O(N^{−12δ/5}),
   309	for δ ≤ 1/96.
   310	
   311	*Proof.* Follow D′ §4(iii), with T = ⌊2^{(1/4−ε′)n}/n⌋. T enters only the analysis.
   312	- **E_a.** By D′ Lemma 8, |E_a| ≤ (T(T+1)/(n−1))·(P/2+1). Dividing by |S_n| ≥ c₀P²/n² gives
   313	  O(n·T²/P) = O(2^{−2ε′n}/n).
   314	- **E_b.** By Theorem 2, n⁴/H⁴ ≤ 2^{−2ε′n}, and n⁶H^{1/3}N^{−1/6} is far smaller.
   315	- **E_c.** D′ bounds it by C·n·(H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T + H^{10/3}/P^{1/3}).
   316	  - H^{14/3} ≤ (2n)^{14/3}2^{7ε′n/3} and P^{1/3}/T ≤ 2n·2^{(ε′−1/12)n}. So
   317	    H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T ≤ (2n)^{6}·2^{(10ε′/3 − 1/12)n}.
   318	  - We need 10ε′/3 − 1/12 < −2ε′, i.e. 16ε′/3 < 1/12, i.e. ε′ < 1/64. For ε′ ≤ 1/80 the
   319	    margin is 1/12 − 16/240 = 1/60. So this term is O(2^{−2ε′n − n/60}·n^{O(1)}).
   320	  - The second term is O(2^{(5ε′/3−1/6)n}).
   321	- **Scale separations.** D′ needs Y > H² and Y⁴ > √2P. Theorem 1 needs Y ≥ 16H² and
   322	  HX < Y⁴. All hold for ε′ ≤ 1/80.
   323	- Cost and correctness are D′(i)–(ii) verbatim. ∎
   324	
   325	**Corollary 3′ (polylog regime).** With H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉, the cost is N^{1/6}(log N)^{O(1+a)}.
   326	The exceptional fraction is O(min(n^{−4a}, n^{−1−2a})), plus D″'s E_a and E_c terms, which are
   327	o(n^{−4a}). D″ had O(n^{−2a}).
