   325	**Corollary 3′ (polylog regime).** With H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉, the cost is N^{1/6}(log N)^{O(1+a)}.
   326	The exceptional fraction is O(min(n^{−4a}, n^{−1−2a})), plus D″'s E_a and E_c terms, which are
   327	o(n^{−4a}). D″ had O(n^{−2a}).
   328	
   329	## 4. Barrier analysis
   330	
   331	### 4.1 An information barrier (Proposition 5; proved, using PYY 2011)
   332	
   333	**Proposition 5.**
   334	- Let 𝒫 be any band and π_ℓ ∈ (0, 1/2] with max π_ℓ ≤ 2 min π_ℓ and Σπ_ℓ = λ.
   335	- For every fixed J ≥ 2 there are c_J > 0 and λ_J such that for λ ≥ λ_J there exist
   336	  J-wise independent Bernoulli variables (X_ℓ)_{ℓ∈𝒫} with P(X_ℓ = 1) = π_ℓ and
   337	
   338	  P(X_ℓ = 0 for all ℓ) ≥ c_J·λ^{−⌊J/2⌋}.
   339	
   340	*Proof.*
   341	- Let π* = max π_ℓ, n′ = |𝒫| and μ = n′π* ≤ 2λ.
   342	- Peled–Yadin–Yehudayoff, "The maximal probability that k-wise independent bits are all 1",
   343	  Random Struct. Alg. 38 (2011) 502–525 (arXiv 0801.0059), handle even k:
   344	  - Theorem 1.1 / Corollary 1.2(1): for fixed even k and N := n′p(1−p) − 1 → ∞, the maximum
   345	    M(n′,k,p) of P(all ones) over k-wise independent bits with marginal p satisfies
   346	    M ≥ (c/k)·p^{n′}/P(Bin(n′,1−p) ≤ k/2).
   347	  - Their (1.1), from Benjamini–Gurel-Gurevich–Peled, handles odd k: M(n′,k+1,p) = p·M(n′−1,k,p).
   348	- Take p = 1 − π*, so N ≈ μ → ∞. Then P(Bin ≤ k/2) ≤ p^{n′}Σ_{i≤k/2}(2μ)^i/i!, so
   349	  M ≥ c_k μ^{−k/2}.
   350	- Complementing the bits gives J-wise independent Y_ℓ with P(Y_ℓ=1) = π* and
   351	  P(all Y = 0) ≥ c_J μ^{−⌊J/2⌋}.
   352	- Thin independently: X_ℓ = Y_ℓ·η_ℓ with η_ℓ ~ Bernoulli(π_ℓ/π*). Any ≤ J of the X_ℓ are
   353	  independent with the right marginals, and P(all X = 0) ≥ P(all Y = 0). ∎
   354	
   355	For prime pairs, attach to each X_ℓ = 1 an independent uniform label v ∈ V. All labelled
   356	(ℓ,v)-event statistics of order ≤ J then match the independent-residue model exactly, while
   357	the uncovered mass is ≥ c_J λ^{−⌊J/2⌋}.
   358	
   359	So **no argument whose only input is the joint distribution of ≤ J band-prime events (moments,
   360	Selberg/Bonferroni majorants, any polynomial of degree ≤ J in the event indicators) can prove
   361	o(λ^{−⌊J/2⌋})**, even with that input known exactly.
   362	
   363	The matching upper side is Selberg's: the degree-2m square majorant gives
   364	(Σ_{i≤m} λ^i/i!)^{−1} ~ m!/λ^m. That is the Christoffel number of the Poisson law, i.e.
   365	PYY's M̃.
   366	
   367	e^{−cλ} by this route needs J ≍ λ, i.e. lattices of index Y^{cλ}. That is astronomically
   368	beyond the box volume X² ≈ Y⁶H².
   369	
   370	### 4.2 Which J the box gives (precise computation; not a theorem about all methods)
   371	
   372	- Theorem 1's machinery works for any j. The normalised error of order j, divided by X²λ^j, is
   373	  dominated by:
   374	  - B_j: ≈ (2Y)^{j/2}2^j/(Xs^j);
   375	  - A_j: (|𝒫|/s)^j/X² ≈ (2Y)^j/X².
   376	- **j = 4, 5:** both are N^{−c}. So joint statistics of ≤ 5 primes are available.
   377	  - By Proposition 5 (J = 5 gives ⌊5/2⌋ = 2), the fifth moment cannot improve λ^{−2}.
   378	  - The same holds for odd-degree majorants: tiny masses far out absorb odd moments.
   379	- **j = 6:** Y⁶ ≈ X²/(2H²), so a typical 6-prime lattice has ≈ 2H² points. The two error
   380	  sources are then:
   381	  - A₆/X² ≈ 64Y⁶/X² ≈ 32/H². Each lattice count is an integer, so an O(1) error per lattice
   382	    cannot be removed lattice by lattice. There are ≈ (λY)⁶·(const) lattices.
   383	  - B₆/X² ≈ 10⁴/(H s⁶) ≈ n⁶/H.
   384	  - Both must be ≪ λ^{−3} ≈ n³/H⁶ for a λ^{−3} bound. They are larger by factors ≥ H⁴/n³.
   385	- So a sixth-moment proof needs the **signed** discrepancies of ≈ (λY)⁶ lattices, each of
   386	  size Θ(1), to cancel to o(n³/H⁴) per lattice on average.
   387	- **Where those discrepancies live (heuristic, not proved).**
   388	  - With smooth weights, a generic 6-prime lattice has negligible error. Its dual vectors
   389	    have length ≈ H/P.
   390	  - All the error sits on *elongated* lattices, those with a vector u of length ≤ d/X ≈ Y³/H.
   391	    These are a ≈ 1/H² fraction, and each has count 0 or ≈ 2H².
   392	  - u ∈ Λ_c says exactly that the events of c hold at the point u. So counting elongated
   393	    lattices is the coverage problem again, at the smaller scale X/H², with a worse
   394	    level-to-volume ratio.
   395	  - The signs depend on where one lattice line falls relative to the box. Their cancellation
   396	    is an equidistribution statement of the same type at the next scale.
   397	  - This is the regress that level methods cannot break.
   398	- GPT-6.1-sol (sieve2d_1, its (24)) gets the same j = 6 failure with the same factor.
   399	
   400	**Fake distribution realised.** Proposition 5 gives a distribution with exact ≤5-prime
   401	statistics and uncovered mass ≍ λ^{−2}. Since the box supplies only ≤5-prime statistics
   402	(to accuracy N^{−c}), the bound λ^{−2} is sharp for every method that uses only them.
   403	
   404	### 4.3 Standard sieve tools, quantified
   405	
   406	- **Fundamental lemma / β-sieve.**
   407	  - The sieve dimension is κ ≈ |V|/2 ≈ 0.3H²: ω(ℓ) = |V| on a band of log-length log 2, and
   408	    half of all primes are ≡ 3 (mod 4).
   409	  - An error factor e^{−s} needs s = log D/log z ≳ κ, which is ≳ λ·log Y.
   410	  - The box offers s ≤ log(X²)/log(2Y) ≈ 6 (1D: ≈ 3).
   411	  - The upper-bound sieve below the sieving limit gives at best Selberg's 1/G(√D). With the
   412	    *effective* level, D such that (|𝒫||V|)^{ω(d)} × O(1) ≤ X²λ^{−ω(d)/2}, that is ω(d) ≤ 5,
   413	    hence again λ^{−2}.
   414	- **Janson / Suen.** They need a product space or a dependency graph. Here all events depend on
   415	  one point (p,q), and Proposition 5 shows that low-order correlations alone cannot imply the
   416	  Janson conclusion.
   417	- **BV / BDH.** Theorem 1 never needs primes in progressions; integers suffice. The obstacle at
   418	  j = 6 is the lattice discrepancy itself, not the prime restriction. Moduli d ≈ Y⁶ ≈ P²/H² are
   419	  in any case far beyond BV's √P range in one variable.
   420	
   421	### 4.4 Primes versus integers
   422	
   423	- **Experimentally they agree** (P4 ratio 1.00004).
   424	- **Theorem 1 loses n²** by bounding prime pairs by all integer points. A Selberg presieve
   425	  inside the fourth moment should give C/λ² for S_n (not done; **conjectured**). It changes
   426	  only polylog factors and none of the exponents above.
   427	- **Qualitatively, primality must matter for any e^{−cλ} proof.**
   428	  - The dilates x = t(H, H+1) with gcd(t, ∏𝒫) = 1 are uncovered integer points. Indeed
   429	    F_v(x) = t(BH − A(H+1)) with 0 < |BH − A(H+1)| ≤ H² < Y.
   430	  - There are ≫ P/H of them in the balanced region.

